<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="Math234Sp22-Midterm2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Math 234 Spring 2022 Midterm 2</title>

	<exercise points="10">
		<statement>
			<p>
				Consider the function <m>f(x, y) = e^{x^2+4y}</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<task points="5">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find a unit vector that points in the direction of maximum increase at <m>(1, 2)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					We need to find a unit vector with direction <m>\nabla f(1, 2)</m>.
					<me>f_x = e^{x^2+4y} \cdot 2x, \quad f_y = e^{x^2+4y} \cdot 4.</me>
					<me>f_x(1, 2) = e^9 \cdot 2, \quad f_y(1, 2) = e^9 \cdot 4.</me>
					<me>\nabla f(1, 2) = \langle 2e^9, 4e^9 \rangle.</me>
					The magnitude is:
					<me>|\nabla f(1, 2)| = \sqrt{4e^{18} + 16e^{18}} = \sqrt{20e^{18}} = \sqrt{20}e^9.</me>
					The unit vector is:
					<me>\hat{u} = \left\langle \frac{2}{\sqrt{20}}, \frac{4}{\sqrt{20}} \right\rangle.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="5">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the directional derivative of <m>f</m> at <m>(1, 2)</m> in the direction of the vector <m>\vec{v} = \langle 4, 3 \rangle</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>|\vec{v}| = \sqrt{16+9} = 5, \quad \hat{v} = \langle 4/5, 3/5 \rangle.</me>
					<me>D_{\vec{v}}f(1, 2) = \langle 2e^9, 4e^9 \rangle \cdot \langle 4/5, 3/5 \rangle = \frac{8}{5}e^9 + \frac{12}{5}e^9 = \frac{20}{5}e^9 = 4e^9.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<exercise points="10">
		<statement>
			<p>
				Let <m>z = \sqrt{x^2+6y^2}</m> where <m>x = f(s, t)</m> and <m>y = g(s, t)</m> are both functions of <m>s</m> and <m>t</m>. Suppose that:
				<me>f(2, 1) = 1, \quad g(2, 1) = 2,</me>
				<me>f_s(2, 1) = 6, \quad g_s(2, 1) = -3,</me>
				<me>f_t(2, 1) = -2, \quad g_t(2, 1) = 1.</me>
				Compute <m>\frac{\partial z}{\partial t}</m> when <m>s = 2</m> and <m>t = 1</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				By the Chain Rule:
				<me>\frac{dz}{dt} = \frac{\partial z}{\partial x} \frac{\partial x}{\partial t} + \frac{\partial z}{\partial y} \frac{\partial y}{\partial t}.</me>
				Compute partials of <m>z</m>:
				<me>\frac{\partial z}{\partial x} = \frac{1}{2}(x^2+6y^2)^{-1/2} \cdot 2x, \quad \frac{\partial z}{\partial y} = \frac{1}{2}(x^2+6y^2)^{-1/2} \cdot 12y.</me>
				When <m>s=2</m> and <m>t=1</m>, we have <m>x = f(2, 1) = 1</m> and <m>y = g(2, 1) = 2</m>.
				Then:
				<me>\frac{\partial z}{\partial x} = \frac{1}{2}(1+24)^{-1/2} \cdot 2 = \frac{1}{5}.</me>
				<me>\frac{\partial z}{\partial y} = \frac{1}{2}(1+24)^{-1/2} \cdot 24 = \frac{12}{5}.</me>
				Using <m>f_t(2,1) = -2</m> and <m>g_t(2,1) = 1</m>:
				<me>\frac{dz}{dt} = \frac{1}{5}(-2) + \frac{12}{5}(1) = \frac{10}{5} = 2.</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise points="10">
		<statement>
			<p>
				Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface <m>z = \sin(x+y) + x^2y</m> at the point <m>(2, -2, -8)</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				The equation of the tangent plane to <m>z = f(x, y)</m> at <m>(2, -2, -8)</m> is:
				<me>z + 8 = f_x(2, -2)(x-2) + f_y(2, -2)(y+2).</me>
				Computing the partial derivatives:
				<me>f_x = \cos(x+y) + 2xy, \quad f_y = \cos(x+y) + x^2.</me>
				Evaluating at <m>(2, -2)</m>:
				<me>f_x(2, -2) = \cos(2-2) + 2(2)(-2) = 1 - 8 = -7.</me>
				<me>f_y(2, -2) = \cos(2-2) + 4 = 5.</me>
				The equation of the tangent plane is:
				<me>z+8 = -7(x-2) + 5(y+2).</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise points="26">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Extrema analysis for <m>f(x, y) = x^2 - xy + y^2</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task points="12">
			<introduction>
				<p>
					Find the maximum value of <m>f(x, y) = x^2 - xy + y^2</m> on the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 2</m>.
				</p>
			</introduction>
			<task points="4">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Using the method of Lagrange Multipliers, what system of equations do you need to solve?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Let <m>g(x, y) = x^2 + y^2 - 2</m>. Solve <m>\nabla f = \lambda \nabla g</m> with <m>g = 0</m>:
						<me>
							\begin{cases}
							2x - y = \lambda \cdot 2x \amp (1) \\
							-x + 2y = \lambda \cdot 2y \amp (2) \\
							x^2 + y^2 = 2 \amp (3)
							\end{cases}
						</me>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
			<task points="4">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Show that the Lagrange multiplier value is either <m>\lambda = 1/2</m> or <m>\lambda = 3/2</m>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						We can write (1) as <m>2x(1-\lambda) = y</m> and (2) as <m>2y(1-\lambda) = x</m>.
						Notice that <m>\lambda \neq 1</m>, as (1) would imply <m>y=0</m> and (2) <m>x=0</m>, but <m>(0,0)</m> is not on the circle.
						Then <m>y = \frac{x}{2(1-\lambda)}</m>. Substituting into (1):
						<me>2x(1-\lambda) = \frac{x}{2(1-\lambda)} \implies 4x(1-\lambda)^2 = x.</me>
						Since <m>x=0</m> implies <m>y=0</m> (which fails), we must have <m>4(1-\lambda)^2 = 1</m>.
						<me>(1-\lambda)^2 = 1/4 \implies 1-\lambda = \pm 1/2 \implies \lambda = 1/2 \text{ or } \lambda = 3/2.</me>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
			<task points="4">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Determine the maximum value of <m>f</m> on the circle.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						<ul>
							<li>Case 1: <m>\lambda = 1/2</m>. Then <m>y = x</m>. (3) implies <m>2x^2 = 2 \implies x = \pm 1</m>. Points <m>(1, 1), (-1, -1)</m>.</li>
							<li>Case 2: <m>\lambda = 3/2</m>. Then <m>y = -x</m>. (3) implies <m>2x^2 = 2 \implies x = \pm 1</m>. Points <m>(1, -1), (-1, 1)</m>.</li>
						</ul>
						Values: <m>f(1,1) = 1, f(-1,-1) = 1, f(-1,1) = 3, f(1,-1) = 3</m>.
						The maximum is 3 and the minimum is 1.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</task>
		<task points="10">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the critical points of <m>f(x, y) = x^2 - xy + y^2</m>, and determine whether they are local maxima, minima or saddle points.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Solve <m>f_x = 2x - y = 0</m> and <m>f_y = -x + 2y = 0</m>.
					From <m>y=2x</m>, we get <m>-x + 4x = 0 \implies x = 0, y = 0</m>.
					Critical point: <m>(0, 0)</m>.
					Second derivative test: <m>f_{xx} = 2, f_{yy} = 2, f_{xy} = -1</m>.
					<m>D = 2 \cdot 2 - (-1)^2 = 3 > 0</m>.
					Since <m>f_{xx} = 2 > 0</m>, <m>(0, 0)</m> is a <strong>local min</strong>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="4">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the maximum and minimum values of <m>f(x, y) = x^2 - xy + y^2</m> in the region <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 2</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The candidate points are the critical point <m>(0, 0)</m> and points on the boundary from part (a): <m>(1, 1), (-1, -1), (1, -1), (-1, 1)</m>.
					Values: <m>f(0, 0) = 0, f(1, 1) = 1, f(-1, -1) = 1, f(-1, 1) = 3, f(1, -1) = 3</m>.
					So the <strong>maximum is 3</strong> and the <strong>minimum is 0</strong>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<exercise points="16">
		<task points="8">
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\displaystyle\iint_D x^2 \, dA</m>, where <m>D</m> is the triangular region with vertices <m>(0, 0), (0, 6)</m> and <m>(3, 3)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The region is bounded by <m>x=0</m>, <m>y=x</m>, and the line through <m>(0, 6)</m> and <m>(3, 3)</m> which is <m>y-3 = -(x-3) \implies y = -x+6</m>.
					<me>\int_0^3 \int_x^{-x+6} x^2 \, dy \, dx = \int_0^3 x^2 y |_{x}^{-x+6} \, dx = \int_0^3 x^2(-x+6-x) \, dx.</me>
					<me>= \int_0^3 x^2(-2x+6) \, dx = \int_0^3 -2x^3 + 6x^2 \, dx.</me>
					<me>= \left[ -\frac{2x^4}{4} + \frac{6x^3}{3} \right]_0^3 = -\frac{1}{2}(81) + 2(27) = -40.5 + 54 = 13.5.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="8">
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\displaystyle\iint_{\mathcal{R}} e^{x^2+y^2} \, dA</m>, where <m>\mathcal{R}</m> is the region in the first quadrant that lies between the circles <m>x^2 + y^2 = 4</m> and <m>x^2 + y^2 = 9</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Use polar coordinates: <m>2 \le r \le 3, 0 \le \theta \le \pi/2</m>.
					<me>\int_0^{\pi/2} \int_2^3 e^{r^2} \cdot r \, dr \, d\theta.</me>
					Let <m>u = r^2</m>, then <m>du = 2r \, dr</m>.
					<me>= \frac{\pi}{2} \int_4^9 \frac{1}{2} e^u \, du = \frac{\pi}{4} [e^u]_4^9 = \frac{\pi}{4}(e^9 - e^4).</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<exercise points="10">
		<statement>
			<p>
				Let <m>A = \displaystyle\int_0^1 \int_{\sqrt{y}}^1 \sqrt{x^3 + 4} \, dx \, dy</m>.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<task points="4">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Give a detailed sketch of the region of integration.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The region is defined by <m>0 \le y \le 1</m> and <m>\sqrt{y} \le x \le 1</m>. This corresponds to the area below <m>y=x^2</m>, from <m>x=0</m> to <m>x=1</m>, and above the <m>x</m>-axis.
					Boundary curves: <m>y = x^2</m>, <m>x = 1</m>, <m>y = 0</m>. Intersection at <m>(0, 0), (1, 1), (1, 0)</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="4">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Construct an equivalent integral for <m>A</m> by reversing the order of integration.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>A = \int_0^1 \int_0^{x^2} \sqrt{x^3 + 4} \, dy \, dx.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="2">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Compute <m>A</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>A = \int_0^1 y \sqrt{x^3+4} |_{0}^{x^2} \, dx = \int_0^1 x^2 \sqrt{x^3+4} \, dx.</me>
					Let <m>u = x^3+4</m>, then <m>du = 3x^2 \, dx</m>.
					<me>= \int_4^5 \frac{1}{3} u^{1/2} \, du = \frac{1}{3} \left[ \frac{u^{3/2}}{3/2} \right]_4^5 = \frac{2}{9} (5^{3/2} - 4^{3/2}).</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<exercise points="18">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Let <m>E</m> be the region in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m> bounded by the surfaces <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 1</m> and <m>z^2 = x^2 + y^2</m> with <m>z \ge 0</m> and <m>y \ge 0</m>. Express the volume of <m>E</m> as an iterated (triple) integral using:
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task points="6">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Cartesian (aka rectangular) coordinates.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The intersection occurs where <m>2z^2 = 1 \implies z = 1/\sqrt{2}</m>, and <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1/2</m>.
					<me>V = \int_{-1/\sqrt{2}}^{1/\sqrt{2}} \int_{0}^{\sqrt{1/2-x^2}} \int_{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}^{\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}} 1 \, dz \, dy \, dx.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="6">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Cylindrical coordinates.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The region <m>y \ge 0</m> corresponds to <m>0 \le \theta \le \pi</m>. The radius goes to <m>1/\sqrt{2}</m>.
					<me>V = \int_0^{\pi} \int_{0}^{1/\sqrt{2}} \int_{r}^{\sqrt{1-r^2}} r \, dz \, dr \, d\theta.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="6">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Spherical coordinates.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The cone <m>z^2 = x^2+y^2</m> is <m>\phi = \pi/4</m>. "Inside" the sphere means <m>0 \le \rho \le 1</m>.
					<me>V = \int_0^{\pi} \int_{0}^{\pi/4} \int_{0}^{1} \rho^2 \sin \phi \, d\rho \, d\phi \, d\theta.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
</worksheet>
